1
00:00:03,400 --> 00:00:05,266
I'm Peppa Pig. Oink!

2
00:00:05,333 --> 00:00:08,033
-This is my little brother George.
-Oink, oink!

3
00:00:08,100 --> 00:00:10,333
This is Mommy Pig. Oink!

4
00:00:10,400 --> 00:00:12,333
And this is Daddy Pig. Oink!

5
00:00:12,399 --> 00:00:13,266
[laughing]

6
00:00:14,133 --> 00:00:15,766
Peppa Pig. Oink!

7
00:00:16,599 --> 00:00:17,466
Gym Class.

8
00:00:17,533 --> 00:00:18,400
[horn honks]

9
00:00:18,466 --> 00:00:21,533
[narrator] Peppa and her friends
are going to the gym today.

10
00:00:22,733 --> 00:00:25,799
Let's check
you've all got your gym kits on.

11
00:00:26,033 --> 00:00:28,766
[narrator]
Pedro is wearing a superhero costume.

12
00:00:29,200 --> 00:00:31,266
Pedro, where is your gym kit?

13
00:00:31,333 --> 00:00:36,033
It's at home.
I thought it was dressing-up day.

14
00:00:36,466 --> 00:00:38,333
Follow me, children.

15
00:00:39,033 --> 00:00:40,066
[narrator] This is the gym,

16
00:00:40,133 --> 00:00:44,133
where mommies and daddies come
to exercise and enjoy themselves.

17
00:00:44,199 --> 00:00:46,033
Are you all having fun?

18
00:00:46,100 --> 00:00:48,300
Yes, lots of fun.

19
00:00:49,533 --> 00:00:53,333
Your gym teacher for today
is Grampy Rabbit.

20
00:00:53,400 --> 00:00:54,566
Where is he?

21
00:00:54,633 --> 00:00:58,733
Hello, children.
Are you ready to exercise?

22
00:00:58,800 --> 00:01:00,766
Yes, Grampy Rabbit.

23
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:03,099
[yells]

24
00:01:04,433 --> 00:01:06,533
Do we have to do that?

25
00:01:06,600 --> 00:01:12,333
No, that's only for big athletes like me.
Not for little explorers like you.

26
00:01:12,399 --> 00:01:14,366
We're not explorers.

27
00:01:14,433 --> 00:01:16,566
I'll make explorers of you.

28
00:01:16,633 --> 00:01:20,366
But you. You look like a super hero.

29
00:01:20,433 --> 00:01:22,500
Uh... It's just pretend.

30
00:01:22,566 --> 00:01:25,033
Pretending, that's good.

31
00:01:25,100 --> 00:01:29,433
Right, let's warm up.
Everyone, run on the spot.

32
00:01:29,500 --> 00:01:30,466
[laughing]

33
00:01:30,533 --> 00:01:32,466
And flap your arms.

34
00:01:33,666 --> 00:01:37,699
Now, stretch, stretch.

35
00:01:38,366 --> 00:01:40,000
Wobble like a jelly.

36
00:01:40,066 --> 00:01:41,233
[burbling]

37
00:01:41,300 --> 00:01:44,333
[all laughing, then burbling]

38
00:01:45,066 --> 00:01:47,300
And... rest.

39
00:01:47,366 --> 00:01:50,500
Now we're all warmed up. We can start.

40
00:01:50,566 --> 00:01:54,000
-Start what?
-Your adventure.

41
00:01:54,066 --> 00:01:58,500
I want you to pretend
that this room is a jungle.

42
00:01:58,566 --> 00:02:00,800
It doesn't look like a jungle.

43
00:02:01,033 --> 00:02:04,233
-Where are the trees?
-You have to imagine it.

44
00:02:04,300 --> 00:02:08,733
The trees, the rain,
the fast flowing river at your feet.

45
00:02:08,799 --> 00:02:14,266
Your first adventure is to walk
on this beam, across the river.

46
00:02:14,733 --> 00:02:16,466
Oh! That's easy.

47
00:02:16,533 --> 00:02:20,266
Ah! But it's night time, and it's windy.

48
00:02:20,333 --> 00:02:21,633
[imitates wind blowing]

49
00:02:21,700 --> 00:02:22,633
What?

50
00:02:22,700 --> 00:02:27,633
That's how it was for me.
It was a dark and stormy night.

51
00:02:29,100 --> 00:02:31,133
I was on an adventure.

52
00:02:34,799 --> 00:02:35,733
Ready?

53
00:02:35,799 --> 00:02:40,000
[bleats] But it's not windy or night time.

54
00:02:40,066 --> 00:02:42,266
You have to pretend.

55
00:02:44,433 --> 00:02:47,500
[narrator]
Everyone crosses the pretend river safely.

56
00:02:47,566 --> 00:02:49,133
Hooray!

57
00:02:49,600 --> 00:02:52,600
Well done, my little explorers.

58
00:02:52,666 --> 00:02:54,766
What's next, Grampy Rabbit?

59
00:02:55,000 --> 00:03:01,033
Your next adventure is to swing across
this swamp full of crocodiles.

60
00:03:01,100 --> 00:03:02,266
[in unison] Ah!

61
00:03:02,333 --> 00:03:04,733
I can't see any crocodiles.

62
00:03:04,799 --> 00:03:06,733
You've got to pretend.

63
00:03:06,799 --> 00:03:10,233
Let those crocodiles know who's boss.

64
00:03:10,299 --> 00:03:13,500
Go away, you naughty crocodiles.

65
00:03:13,566 --> 00:03:15,333
That's the spirit.

66
00:03:15,399 --> 00:03:18,533
Did you ever swing across
a crocodile swamp?

67
00:03:18,600 --> 00:03:20,200
Of course I did.

68
00:03:20,266 --> 00:03:22,000
Were you scared?

69
00:03:22,066 --> 00:03:24,633
Not as scared as they were of me.

70
00:03:27,333 --> 00:03:30,299
[yells]

71
00:03:33,366 --> 00:03:36,000
Ready? Don't forget to do the call.

72
00:03:36,066 --> 00:03:39,000
[all yelling]

73
00:03:39,066 --> 00:03:42,766
[narrator] Everyone has crossed
the pretend crocodile swamp safely.

74
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:44,100
Well done.

75
00:03:44,166 --> 00:03:46,500
What's our next adventure?

76
00:03:46,566 --> 00:03:51,600
This is a vault.
Big athletes jump over it, like this.

77
00:03:52,133 --> 00:03:53,299
Ooh!

78
00:03:53,366 --> 00:03:58,299
But you can pretend it's a dinosaur
and crawl through its legs.

79
00:03:58,366 --> 00:04:02,299
Have you crawled through
a dinosaur's legs, Grampy Rabbit?

80
00:04:02,366 --> 00:04:05,566
Oh, yes.
Once, I was walking in the jungle,

81
00:04:05,633 --> 00:04:09,166
when I stumbled
into a lost world of dinosaurs.

82
00:04:09,233 --> 00:04:10,566
[roars]

83
00:04:13,766 --> 00:04:15,766
[giggling]

84
00:04:16,800 --> 00:04:20,466
And that's it.
You've made it out of the jungle.

85
00:04:20,533 --> 00:04:21,500
Hooray!

86
00:04:21,566 --> 00:04:22,433
Ah-ho!

87
00:04:23,033 --> 00:04:25,466
Did you have a good gym class, children?

88
00:04:25,533 --> 00:04:27,766
Yes, Madame Gazelle.

89
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:31,000
They're real little explorers now.

90
00:04:31,066 --> 00:04:33,000
We walked across a river.

91
00:04:33,066 --> 00:04:35,366
And we swung over crocodiles.

92
00:04:35,433 --> 00:04:37,766
And we crawled under a dinosaur.

93
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:43,133
I love doing Gym Class
with Grampy Rabbit. Oink!

94
00:04:47,633 --> 00:04:50,500
[Peppa] The Blackberry Bush.

95
00:04:50,566 --> 00:04:53,733
[narrator] Peppa and her family
are at Granny and Grandpa Pig's house.

96
00:04:53,800 --> 00:04:57,433
Oink. Today I'm going to make
apple and blackberry crumble.

97
00:04:57,500 --> 00:04:58,400
Yummy.

98
00:04:58,466 --> 00:05:01,733
I need you brave explorers
to go and get the fruit.

99
00:05:01,800 --> 00:05:03,400
Aye, aye, Granny Pig.

100
00:05:04,133 --> 00:05:09,400
-We've got baskets for the apples.
-And buckets for the blackberries.

101
00:05:10,166 --> 00:05:14,366
Oink. First, the easy bit, picking apples.

102
00:05:14,433 --> 00:05:18,200
On the count of three, shake the tree.

103
00:05:18,266 --> 00:05:21,033
One, two, three.

104
00:05:23,533 --> 00:05:25,533
[giggling]

105
00:05:26,166 --> 00:05:30,800
-Now for the blackberries.
-Why don't we just have apple crumble?

106
00:05:31,033 --> 00:05:34,000
Because blackberries are tasty, too,
Grandpa.

107
00:05:34,066 --> 00:05:35,333
Okay.

108
00:05:35,400 --> 00:05:37,366
Now the hard bit.

109
00:05:39,433 --> 00:05:42,233
Now, that's what I call a blackberry bush.

110
00:05:42,300 --> 00:05:45,333
This bush has been here
since I was a little piggy.

111
00:05:45,400 --> 00:05:50,266
Oink. It's an overgrown weed.
I should have cut it down years ago.

112
00:05:50,333 --> 00:05:53,566
Grandpa, why don't you like it?

113
00:05:53,633 --> 00:05:56,500
I don't like the way it looks at me.

114
00:05:56,566 --> 00:05:59,666
Silly Grandpa. It's just a harmless bush.

115
00:05:59,733 --> 00:06:02,466
And it's covered in lovely blackberries.

116
00:06:03,266 --> 00:06:07,133
[narrator] George has seen
some really big juicy blackberries.

117
00:06:07,200 --> 00:06:08,066
[whines]

118
00:06:08,133 --> 00:06:11,166
Careful, George, or you might get tangled.

119
00:06:11,233 --> 00:06:12,099
[giggles]

120
00:06:12,166 --> 00:06:15,733
I have a stick
for picking hard-to-reach blackberries.

121
00:06:17,033 --> 00:06:19,066
Clever Grandpa.

122
00:06:19,133 --> 00:06:22,700
But the best blackberries
are right at the very top.

123
00:06:22,766 --> 00:06:25,133
That's why I've brought the ladder.

124
00:06:25,200 --> 00:06:28,133
Um... Don't lean too far over, Mommy Pig.

125
00:06:28,200 --> 00:06:33,099
Don't worry. I've been climbing this
blackberry bush since I was a little girl.

126
00:06:33,166 --> 00:06:37,200
-Yes, but you're not a little girl now.
-I know what I'm doing.

127
00:06:37,333 --> 00:06:38,333
[screams]

128
00:06:38,400 --> 00:06:42,433
[narrator] Oh, dear. Mommy Pig has fallen
into the blackberry bush.

129
00:06:42,500 --> 00:06:44,700
Mommy, can you get out?

130
00:06:45,200 --> 00:06:46,733
No. I'm stuck.

131
00:06:46,799 --> 00:06:51,099
You're stuck in a thorny bush
like Sleeping Beauty.

132
00:06:51,166 --> 00:06:52,133
What?

133
00:06:52,200 --> 00:06:57,599
Once upon a time, there was a princess
called Sleeping Beauty.

134
00:06:57,666 --> 00:07:01,700
One day she fell asleep in a thorny bush,

135
00:07:01,766 --> 00:07:05,366
and she stayed there for a hundred years.

136
00:07:05,433 --> 00:07:07,599
Yes, that's a nice story, Peppa.

137
00:07:07,666 --> 00:07:08,533
[bell chimes]

138
00:07:08,599 --> 00:07:09,566
[narrator] It is Suzy Sheep.

139
00:07:09,633 --> 00:07:13,299
-[bleats] Hello, Peppa.
-Oink. Hello, Suzy.

140
00:07:13,366 --> 00:07:16,666
My mommy is in a blackberry bush.

141
00:07:16,733 --> 00:07:19,433
And she'll be there for a hundred years.

142
00:07:19,500 --> 00:07:22,266
Like Sleeping Beauty. [laughs]

143
00:07:22,333 --> 00:07:27,233
And she'll be rescued by a handsome prince
who will give her a kiss.

144
00:07:27,666 --> 00:07:32,066
[giggles] My mommy is having an adventure.

145
00:07:32,133 --> 00:07:35,366
I wish my mommy
would have adventures like that.

146
00:07:35,433 --> 00:07:39,000
Um... Can someone think of a way
to get me out of here?

147
00:07:39,066 --> 00:07:40,433
Don't worry, Mommy.

148
00:07:40,500 --> 00:07:45,433
In a hundred years,
a handsome prince will rescue you.

149
00:07:45,500 --> 00:07:48,099
Oink. I'll be that handsome prince.

150
00:07:48,166 --> 00:07:51,466
Grandpa Pig, may I borrow
your pruning shears, please?

151
00:07:51,533 --> 00:07:55,500
Of course you may,
brave Sir Daddy Pig.

152
00:07:55,566 --> 00:07:57,500
Stand back, everyone.

153
00:07:57,566 --> 00:08:00,233
Take that, you thorny bush, you.

154
00:08:02,366 --> 00:08:03,766
-Yay!
-Aw...

155
00:08:04,033 --> 00:08:06,533
-My prince.
-My princess.

156
00:08:06,599 --> 00:08:07,766
Mmmwah!

157
00:08:08,000 --> 00:08:11,599
You were meant to stay in there
for a hundred years.

158
00:08:11,666 --> 00:08:13,799
That was quite long enough, thank you.

159
00:08:14,033 --> 00:08:17,133
Oink. Mommy is a blackberry bush.

160
00:08:17,733 --> 00:08:20,566
I thought this sort of thing
only happened to me.

161
00:08:20,633 --> 00:08:24,366
Oink! Stand still while we pick you.

162
00:08:24,433 --> 00:08:26,166
[children giggling]

163
00:08:26,233 --> 00:08:29,766
Let's take all this fruit
back to Granny Pig.

164
00:08:31,133 --> 00:08:34,666
Granny. Granny.
Mommy had an adventure.

165
00:08:35,333 --> 00:08:39,633
And then Mommy fell in the bush.
She looked quite silly.

166
00:08:39,699 --> 00:08:42,199
And she was going: Ah!

167
00:08:42,299 --> 00:08:44,166
"Help me. Help me."

168
00:08:44,233 --> 00:08:46,699
And then Daddy came and rescued her.

169
00:08:46,766 --> 00:08:48,366
The end.

170
00:08:48,433 --> 00:08:49,699
Thank you, Peppa.

171
00:08:49,766 --> 00:08:53,100
I think we've all heard that story
enough times now.

172
00:08:53,166 --> 00:08:54,699
But it's funny.

173
00:08:54,766 --> 00:08:59,300
I never want to see
another blackberry in my life.

174
00:08:59,366 --> 00:09:02,566
So, you don't want any
apple and blackberry crumble?

175
00:09:02,633 --> 00:09:03,766
Well...

176
00:09:04,233 --> 00:09:05,566
Mmm!

177
00:09:05,633 --> 00:09:07,033
Delicious.

178
00:09:07,100 --> 00:09:10,433
[narrator] Mummy Pig loves
apple and blackberry crumble.

179
00:09:10,600 --> 00:09:14,000
Everyone loves
Apple and blackberry crumble.

180
00:09:19,400 --> 00:09:20,566
[Peppa] Pottery.

181
00:09:22,300 --> 00:09:25,400
[narrator] Peppa and George are playing
at Zoe Zebra's house.

182
00:09:26,433 --> 00:09:30,199
Little Zuzu and Zaza
are making a jungle with play-clay.

183
00:09:31,266 --> 00:09:34,033
George has made a dinosaur
out of play-clay.

184
00:09:34,100 --> 00:09:35,066
Grr!

185
00:09:35,133 --> 00:09:37,800
Peppa and Zoë are having a dolls' picnic.

186
00:09:38,033 --> 00:09:42,433
Hello, Mr. Monkey.
Would you like a cup of tea?

187
00:09:42,500 --> 00:09:43,500
Ooh! Ooh! Ooh!

188
00:09:43,566 --> 00:09:45,766
Monkey says yes.

189
00:09:46,000 --> 00:09:49,000
What's the magic word, Mr. Monkey?

190
00:09:49,066 --> 00:09:50,033
Ooh! Ooh! Ooh!

191
00:09:50,100 --> 00:09:52,300
That means "please."

192
00:09:52,366 --> 00:09:55,366
Oh, we haven't got a tea set.

193
00:09:55,433 --> 00:09:59,033
We could make a tea set out of play-clay.

194
00:09:59,100 --> 00:10:00,199
[Peppa] Yes.

195
00:10:00,500 --> 00:10:04,466
George, can we use the play-clay, please?

196
00:10:04,533 --> 00:10:06,333
No. Grr!

197
00:10:06,400 --> 00:10:12,566
Zuzu, Zaza, we need to make a tea set
for our tea party.

198
00:10:12,633 --> 00:10:13,633
-No.
-No.

199
00:10:13,699 --> 00:10:16,233
You can help us make it.

200
00:10:17,166 --> 00:10:18,666
[giggling]

201
00:10:18,733 --> 00:10:20,566
I'll make the teapot.

202
00:10:20,633 --> 00:10:23,066
And we'll make the cups.

203
00:10:26,199 --> 00:10:29,266
There. Now Monkey can have tea.

204
00:10:29,600 --> 00:10:32,733
Oh, this teapot is too soft.

205
00:10:32,800 --> 00:10:35,666
Real tea sets aren't soft.

206
00:10:36,166 --> 00:10:39,266
My mommy makes real pots.

207
00:10:39,333 --> 00:10:42,266
-She does pottery.
-Ooh!

208
00:10:44,300 --> 00:10:45,433
Mommy.

209
00:10:45,500 --> 00:10:48,466
Can you help us make a tea set, please?

210
00:10:48,533 --> 00:10:53,666
With tea cups and a teapot
that we can put real water in.

211
00:10:53,733 --> 00:10:55,633
Okay. Come with me.

212
00:10:56,266 --> 00:10:58,800
[announcer] This is where Mrs. Zebra
makes her pottery.

213
00:10:59,033 --> 00:11:00,800
Let's make this tea set.

214
00:11:01,666 --> 00:11:04,333
-Oink. What's that?
-Clay.

215
00:11:04,400 --> 00:11:06,633
All pottery starts off like that.

216
00:11:06,699 --> 00:11:09,733
But it's soft, like play-clay.

217
00:11:09,800 --> 00:11:14,733
We need it to be soft so we can shape it.
But later, it won't be soft.

218
00:11:14,800 --> 00:11:16,000
Ooh!

219
00:11:16,400 --> 00:11:20,199
[narrator] Mommy Zebra is making
a little cup on the potter's wheel.

220
00:11:21,100 --> 00:11:24,199
-There. One cup.
-Wow!

221
00:11:24,266 --> 00:11:27,466
Oink. That looks easy.

222
00:11:27,533 --> 00:11:30,733
-Would you like to try?
-Yes, please.

223
00:11:32,333 --> 00:11:35,800
[giggles] It's all slimy.

224
00:11:38,233 --> 00:11:41,400
Ah! It's all gone wrong.

225
00:11:41,466 --> 00:11:44,766
[narrator] Making a cup
on a potter's wheel is not easy.

226
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:48,100
There is an easier way to make a cup.

227
00:11:48,166 --> 00:11:52,199
First we roll the clay
into a long worm, like this.

228
00:11:52,266 --> 00:11:55,066
Oink. It's a wiggly worm.

229
00:11:55,133 --> 00:11:59,466
♪ I'm a wiggly worm
I'm a wiggly worm ♪

230
00:11:59,533 --> 00:12:04,133
♪ I'm made of clay
What shall I make today? ♪

231
00:12:04,199 --> 00:12:06,233
♪ I'm a wiggly worm ♪

232
00:12:09,233 --> 00:12:12,000
[Mrs. Zebra]
We wrap it round and round like this.

233
00:12:12,066 --> 00:12:14,300
It's a bit bumpy.

234
00:12:14,366 --> 00:12:17,600
Now I smooth it out with my hands.

235
00:12:17,666 --> 00:12:20,266
-And I add a handle.
-[giggles]

236
00:12:20,333 --> 00:12:22,066
It's a cup.

237
00:12:22,133 --> 00:12:24,666
Now we have two cups.

238
00:12:24,733 --> 00:12:28,466
Yes. But a tea set needs
more than two cups.

239
00:12:28,533 --> 00:12:31,400
-We'll all make cups.
-Yes.

240
00:12:31,466 --> 00:12:33,466
[giggling]

241
00:12:36,099 --> 00:12:37,766
Dinosaur.

242
00:12:38,000 --> 00:12:40,500
George has made a dinosaur.

243
00:12:40,566 --> 00:12:44,133
George always makes dinosaurs.

244
00:12:44,199 --> 00:12:46,766
-Grr!
-Oh, what a scary dinosaur.

245
00:12:48,633 --> 00:12:52,133
Now we need to bake the tea set in a kiln.

246
00:12:53,466 --> 00:12:57,500
[narrator] A kiln is an oven
that cooks the clay and makes it hard.

247
00:12:59,133 --> 00:13:00,000
[chimes]

248
00:13:00,066 --> 00:13:02,699
-The tea set should be ready now.
-Hooray!

249
00:13:02,766 --> 00:13:04,500
Now we paint them.

250
00:13:05,133 --> 00:13:08,733
[narrator] The children are painting
the tea set lovely bright colors.

251
00:13:09,800 --> 00:13:11,466
The tea set is finished.

252
00:13:11,533 --> 00:13:14,466
Now we can have our tea party.

253
00:13:14,533 --> 00:13:17,133
There's a cup for everyone.

254
00:13:17,199 --> 00:13:20,500
Oh, we forgot to make a teapot.

255
00:13:20,566 --> 00:13:24,466
I know. George's dinosaur
can be the teapot.

256
00:13:25,133 --> 00:13:28,300
Yes. Let's fill it with water.

257
00:13:31,300 --> 00:13:32,466
[giggles]

258
00:13:33,199 --> 00:13:36,733
This is the best tea set in the world.

259
00:13:36,800 --> 00:13:40,199
Everybody knows
that all the finest tea sets

260
00:13:40,266 --> 00:13:42,066
have a dinosaur in them.

261
00:13:42,133 --> 00:13:44,033
Teapot. Grr!

262
00:13:50,800 --> 00:13:53,533
[Peppa] Paper Airplanes.

263
00:13:53,599 --> 00:13:56,433
[narrator]
Daddy Pig is getting ready to go to work.

264
00:13:56,500 --> 00:14:00,099
Good. That's all the papers I need. Bye.

265
00:14:00,166 --> 00:14:01,466
Bye-bye, Daddy.

266
00:14:05,333 --> 00:14:08,366
Mommy, can we make something?

267
00:14:08,433 --> 00:14:11,766
-Yes. What would you like to make?
-Dinosaur.

268
00:14:12,000 --> 00:14:17,000
Oh, George.
You always say "dinosaur" for everything.

269
00:14:17,066 --> 00:14:18,800
Let's make an airplane.

270
00:14:19,033 --> 00:14:21,233
-Oink, oink!
-[narrator] George likes airplanes.

271
00:14:21,300 --> 00:14:24,766
Can we make an airplane that flies?

272
00:14:25,000 --> 00:14:28,099
-Yes. All we need is some paper.
-Oink!

273
00:14:28,166 --> 00:14:30,500
We know where there's paper.

274
00:14:30,566 --> 00:14:31,566
[giggling]

275
00:14:33,099 --> 00:14:36,233
[narrator] There is lots of old paper
in the workroom.

276
00:14:36,666 --> 00:14:39,266
-Here's some paper.
-Perfect.

277
00:14:39,333 --> 00:14:42,733
[narrator] Mummy Pig is going
to make a paper airplane.

278
00:14:42,800 --> 00:14:44,699
[Mommy Pig]
Fold the paper down the middle.

279
00:14:46,233 --> 00:14:48,333
Now fold the corners in at one end.

280
00:14:49,033 --> 00:14:51,666
Fold those corners in again
to make a point.

281
00:14:52,400 --> 00:14:54,599
Then fold the sides back like this.

282
00:14:56,066 --> 00:14:58,366
Oink! Paper airplanes.

283
00:14:58,433 --> 00:15:00,133
[imitates plane engine]

284
00:15:00,199 --> 00:15:02,033
Let's see if they can fly.

285
00:15:02,099 --> 00:15:02,800
-Oink!
-Oink, oink!

286
00:15:03,433 --> 00:15:05,599
Ready, steady, go.

287
00:15:06,099 --> 00:15:07,066
[both] Ooh!

288
00:15:08,433 --> 00:15:12,300
[narrator] Mommy Pig's airplane
has flown into a tree.

289
00:15:13,066 --> 00:15:15,433
My turn. Wee!

290
00:15:17,699 --> 00:15:20,599
[narrator] Peppa's airplane has landed
in a flowerpot.

291
00:15:20,666 --> 00:15:23,666
-Your turn, George.
-Wee!

292
00:15:23,733 --> 00:15:26,533
[narrator] George's airplane is doing
a loop the loop.

293
00:15:27,633 --> 00:15:31,233
-And has landed in the duck pond.
-[quacking]

294
00:15:31,300 --> 00:15:34,400
-[giggles]
-Let's make a big airplane.

295
00:15:34,466 --> 00:15:37,199
We'll need a really big piece of paper.

296
00:15:39,133 --> 00:15:42,033
Here's a big piece of blue paper.

297
00:15:43,733 --> 00:15:47,199
[narrator] Peppa and George
have made a big blue airplane.

298
00:15:47,266 --> 00:15:48,199
[horn honks]

299
00:15:48,266 --> 00:15:49,466
Daddy Pig.

300
00:15:49,533 --> 00:15:52,733
-Oink. Hello, everyone.
-Shouldn't you be at work?

301
00:15:52,800 --> 00:15:56,266
Yes, but I've forgotten
some important papers.

302
00:15:56,333 --> 00:15:59,500
Daddy, we're making airplanes.

303
00:15:59,566 --> 00:16:02,566
Oink. We made a really big one.

304
00:16:02,633 --> 00:16:05,000
Wow! Fantastic.

305
00:16:05,066 --> 00:16:07,766
You need someone big and strong
to throw it.

306
00:16:08,400 --> 00:16:11,233
-Ready, steady, go.
-Go!

307
00:16:12,233 --> 00:16:16,599
[narrator] The big blue airplane is flying
very high and very far.

308
00:16:17,233 --> 00:16:20,633
-It just keeps on going.
-Bye-bye, airplane.

309
00:16:20,699 --> 00:16:21,666
[laughs]

310
00:16:21,733 --> 00:16:23,133
Bye-bye.

311
00:16:23,599 --> 00:16:27,566
Now, I need to find
my important work papers.

312
00:16:29,666 --> 00:16:32,266
Has anyone seen any pieces of paper?

313
00:16:32,333 --> 00:16:33,466
-Um...
-Uh...

314
00:16:33,533 --> 00:16:35,500
Maybe you should follow me.

315
00:16:36,666 --> 00:16:40,699
-Is this one of your important papers?
-Yes. That's one of them.

316
00:16:41,733 --> 00:16:45,333
Is this one of your important papers,
Daddy?

317
00:16:45,400 --> 00:16:47,633
Yes. Thank you, Peppa.

318
00:16:47,699 --> 00:16:48,766
[duck quacks]

319
00:16:49,000 --> 00:16:52,466
-Uh... Why are we at the duck pond?
-[quacks]

320
00:16:52,533 --> 00:16:55,000
Oh, I see.

321
00:16:55,066 --> 00:16:56,500
-Thank you, Mrs. Duck.
-[quacks]

322
00:16:56,566 --> 00:17:01,466
The only paper I'm missing now
is a big blueprint.

323
00:17:01,533 --> 00:17:04,133
Hmm... Is it big and blue?

324
00:17:04,200 --> 00:17:08,266
Like the airplane you threw
a long way away, Daddy?

325
00:17:08,333 --> 00:17:10,233
Oh... Um...

326
00:17:10,300 --> 00:17:12,533
The one that we said bye-bye to?

327
00:17:12,599 --> 00:17:15,533
Yes. Maybe I should ring the office.

328
00:17:15,599 --> 00:17:17,200
[phone beeps, then line ringing]

329
00:17:17,266 --> 00:17:19,733
[narrator] This is Daddy Pig's office.

330
00:17:19,800 --> 00:17:21,233
[ringing continues]

331
00:17:21,300 --> 00:17:24,666
Hello, Daddy Pig.
Did you find the papers?

332
00:17:24,733 --> 00:17:26,399
I found some of them.

333
00:17:26,466 --> 00:17:30,066
The only one we need is the big blueprint.

334
00:17:30,133 --> 00:17:34,066
-That might be a problem.
-Daddy threw it away.

335
00:17:34,133 --> 00:17:38,666
-Threw it away?
-[Peppa] And it flew and flew forever.

336
00:17:38,733 --> 00:17:42,333
[yelps]
It's here. It just landed on my desk.

337
00:17:42,399 --> 00:17:43,466
Eh?

338
00:17:43,633 --> 00:17:44,800
What a great idea

339
00:17:45,033 --> 00:17:48,633
to make it into a paper airplane
and throw it to us.

340
00:17:48,700 --> 00:17:54,133
Oh, yes, welI, I am a bit of an expert
at throwing things.

341
00:17:54,200 --> 00:17:56,266
Thank you, Daddy Pig.

342
00:17:56,333 --> 00:17:58,333
[narrator] Now the blueprint is delivered,

343
00:17:58,399 --> 00:18:01,500
Daddy Pig can take
the rest of the day off work.

344
00:18:01,566 --> 00:18:02,700
[sighs]

345
00:18:02,766 --> 00:18:04,066
That's nice.

346
00:18:04,133 --> 00:18:07,133
Daddy, we need your newspaper.

347
00:18:07,200 --> 00:18:11,800
-Oink. What for?
-Making paper airplanes.

348
00:18:12,033 --> 00:18:13,533
[laughing]

